pkgname=syn-install
pkgver=0.1.0
# 79: every package is a checkbox, and there is a shelf of ordinary software.
#   Eleven of our own — synpkg, synfiles, syn-settings, syn-disks, syn-edit,
#   syntty, syn, syn-model, syn-firstboot, syn-confine, fetch — were installed
#   whatever the answer, and Custom could not decline any of them. All of them
#   are rows on a page now; what stays forced is only what pacman would force
#   anyway, and sel_resolve_deps() re-ticks that and says which and why.
#   Also 47 programs from core/extra across five pages, because an installed
#   SynapseOS shipped with NO WEB BROWSER unless the Full preset happened to
#   pull one in as nexus-chat's dependency.
# 78: cliamp in SEL_APPS, beside syn-arcade. Big screen mode's Music tile plays
#   without a window ONLY with that player — every other one opens a window
#   somebody then has to get out of with a gamepad — so a fresh install without
#   it had half of big screen mode described and absent.
# 80: a fresh install boots into SYNAPSE Prism — `theme = prism` and
#   `glass_level = 55` in the synuirc this writes. NOT synui's compiled default,
#   which stays `synapse`: an existing machine that never picked a theme must
#   keep the desktop it has, and an upgrade that silently restyles one is not
#   something this project does. A fresh install has no desktop to preserve.
# 83: three things a GNOME install got wrong, all first seen on one screenshot
#   of its login screen.
#   - The motd is WRITTEN for the installed machine, not copied off the live
#     media. ⚠ A GRAPHICAL LOGIN SHOWS THE motd — pam_motd emits it as a
#     PAM_TEXT_INFO message and GDM renders PAM's messages in the login dialog —
#     so an installed machine was greeting its owner with "No AI model ships on
#     this media" and "To install to disk: syn-install", and naming synui as the
#     compositor on a box installed with GNOME. It now names the desktop that
#     was actually chosen. /etc/issue is still copied; only motd changed.
#   - GDM wore the ARCH logo, because that is what Arch's gdm sets
#     org.gnome.login-screen `logo` to. A dconf system-db drop-in points it at
#     /usr/share/pixmaps/synapseos-logo.svg, and the branding directory is now
#     copied whole rather than logo.txt alone.
#   - GNOME is the SESSION now, not the `gnome` group. The group is 58 packages
#     and a third of them duplicate something SynapseOS ships — the app grid
#     held two file managers, two text editors, two calculators and two
#     software centres. The list names what it keeps and the comment says what
#     it dropped and why. ⚠ kde-applications-meta on the KDE path is the same
#     problem an order of magnitude larger and is deliberately NOT changed here.
# 85: the install verified as FAILED but had succeeded. Reported 2026-08-20 with
#   a screenshot: "Installation failed" over a log that was green to the last
#   step, dying on
#     ✗ a limine entry names a file that is not on the ESP —
#       limine.conf: /<machine-id>/linux/initramfs#1dfefcbeb440…
#   That #<128-hex> is limine's BLAKE2B-512 INTEGRITY PIN, written `path#<hash>`
#   by limine-entry-tool — which the mkinitcpio hook installed one step earlier
#   runs, and which every future kernel runs again. It boots private per-kernel
#   copies under /boot/<machine-id>/<kernel>/, not /boot/vmlinuz-*, and pins each
#   (see reference_limine_entry_dir_goes_stale). The `#<hash>` is limine SYNTAX,
#   not part of the filename: the file on disk is `…/linux/initramfs`.
#   esp_entry_missing_file() tested the whole string with [ -f ], so it looked
#   for a file ending in 128 hex digits — which never exists — and died on every
#   install that reached the hook, over a perfectly bootable system. It now
#   strips the pin (p=${p%%#*}), guards on the boot(): prefix, and also matches
#   `path:` (limine-entry-tool's key for the kernel). layout_test.sh gains a
#   hash-pinned case both ways: files present passes, a genuinely missing file
#   is still caught and reported de-hashed.
# 86: the checkbox page writes its answer to /etc/synapseos/components.conf.
#   syn-update had no way to tell a component you declined from one the tree
#   had gained since, so the first update reinstalled everything you unticked.
# 87: a fresh install did not follow its own theme's glass. The synuirc this
#   writes carried `glass_level = 55` — and a NUMBER is an explicit answer that
#   survives a theme switch, so every one of the twelve non-glass themes was
#   handed Prism's level instead of the opacities it was tuned with, on every
#   machine, permanently. It was redundant too: synui already gives a glass
#   theme SYN_GLASS_PANEL_DEFAULT (the same 55) when nobody sets a level, so
#   this was the second copy of a decision the compositor already makes — and
#   the copy that could be wrong. Now `auto`. Panels and bar are visually
#   identical; windows go from 0.79 to Prism's own 0.90.
#   ⚠ `widget_glass = on`, NOT auto, and the asymmetry is the point: the desktop
#   widgets are quickshell and read theme.state, which synui-apply-theme writes
#   only when somebody PICKS a theme and which refuses to create itself
#   otherwise. A fresh install NAMES a theme and has never picked one, so
#   theme.state does not exist and `auto` leaves the widgets nothing to follow —
#   solid widgets on a glass desktop. synui's own theme.c names this as the
#   answer for exactly this case.
#   `dock_style = auto` written explicitly: already the compiled default, and
#   this file should be readable as what the machine was given.
#   Four checks in layout_test, including that a NUMBER never comes back.
# 91: synstudio is a checkbox. Both surfaces, because they are two rosters:
#   SEL_COMPONENTS in syn-install.sh and the rows table in the GUI. A name in
#   one and not the other is a component the text installer offers and the
#   graphical one silently cannot.
# 92: fifteen open-source typefaces ship with every install. synui's font
#   picker and synstudio's title font list are both plain fontconfig queries —
#   they show what is installed and nothing else — and a stock SynapseOS
#   carried noto-fonts, ttf-dejavu and terminus-font, so "choose the font the
#   desktop is drawn in" offered DejaVu or a console face. A picker with
#   nothing in it is not a setting. Sans, serif and mono, all OFL or Apache,
#   ~88MB on disk. NOT a checkbox: everything on those pages is a program
#   somebody wants or does not, and this is the contents of a menu.
#   ⚠ ITS OWN pacman transaction, NOT folded in with the language pack. That
#   one is load-bearing — without it a non-Latin locale draws every glyph as a
#   box — and one renamed typeface in a shared transaction takes noto-fonts
#   down with it, which is the samsung-m2020 shape. Retried per package,
#   never fatal. TYPEFACE_PKGS sits ABOVE the source-only seam so the test
#   reads the variable rather than scraping the file; seven checks in
#   layout_test, including that the two transactions stay apart.
#   ⚠ The ISO is deliberately NOT grown by them — a live session is not where
#   anybody sets their desktop font, and that file already argues the case
#   against putting 130MB of fonts on every image.
# 93: the house desktop, shipped. A fresh install came up on Prism with the
#   theme's own restrained glass and synui's stock dock, which is not the
#   desktop SynapseOS is: Glass at 100 (bar and dock resolve to
#   SYN_BAR_ALPHA_FROSTED, 0.05), see-through windows at Prism's own 0.90/0.84,
#   a floating-pill bar, a capsule dock, the accent off the wallpaper on every
#   theme, live backdrop ink, and St. Louis at night behind all of it.
#   ⚠ `glass_level = 100` UNDOES 90's "never write a number here", knowingly.
#   That check was right about the cost — an explicit level survives a theme
#   switch, so a user who later picks Gruvbox is handed Prism's glass — and
#   there is no way to ship a glass desktop as the default without paying it,
#   because `auto` IS the more solid desktop. Appearance ▸ Glass ▸ Auto is the
#   one row that hands every surface back. The check now asserts the decision
#   is complete instead of forbidding it.
#   ⚠ bar_opacity and dock_opacity are written out even though the level
#   resolves to both: the bar and the widgets are quickshell and read them out
#   of theme.state → settings.state → synuirc, and a fresh install has neither
#   of the first two. Without the lines the strip came up at 0.72 over a dock
#   the compositor had already drawn at 0.05.
#   ⚠ active_opacity is PINNED and inactive_opacity is not. The window curve at
#   level 100 is 1.00 - 0.38 = 0.62 — the floor of the curve, not a number
#   anybody chose — and it would have overwritten 0.90 at every login. The
#   unfocused row is derived (active - 0.06) and lands on the theme's 0.84.
#   ⚠ The wallpaper is the COLOUR St. Louis and not the noir cut. Prism has no
#   accent of its own worth the name; it measures the picture. A greyscale
#   wallpaper would have booted every fresh install onto the fallback cyan.
#   Also writes rgb.state and the syn-rgb.path user symlink, which is what
#   `syn-rgb on` does and cannot do from a chroot — the state without the unit
#   is a row that says On over a bridge that never runs.
#   ⚠ synui's SYN_GLASS_PANEL_DEFAULT moves to 100 in the same breath (synui
#   458+): it is the level a desktop that reaches Prism through the THEME
#   MANAGER is handed, the same decision written twice, and layout_test now
#   asserts the two files agree. It also asserts the ISO's synuirc matches this
#   heredoc key for key — 0.2.9.2 shipped an image on the neon theme and a disk
#   on Prism, and nothing caught it.
# 94: the fresh-install synuirc goes back to `glass_level = auto`.
#   93 wrote 100 because `auto` did not produce the desktop Prism is drawn for
#   — a workaround for a bug, and one that cost exactly what the check before it
#   was written to prevent: an explicit level survives a theme switch, so a user
#   who later picked Gruvbox was handed Prism's glass. synui 467 fixes both
#   halves (the panels' ladder and the dock having no theme question), so auto
#   and 100 resolve identically on Prism and the number is simply redundant.
#   ⚠ active_opacity AND glass_pinned COME OUT WITH IT. At level 100 the slider
#   drove windows to 1.00 - 0.38 = 0.62, the floor of that curve, so 93 had to
#   write 0.90 down and pin it off the slider. With no level set there is
#   nothing to drive them and both Prisms' own preset pair stands. A pin in a
#   fresh install's synuirc is a row the user never touched claiming to be one
#   they did.
#   ⚠ bar_opacity and dock_opacity STAY, and they are not the level in disguise:
#   the bar and the widgets are quickshell and read them out of theme.state ->
#   settings.state -> synuirc, and a fresh install has neither of the first two.
#   Same gap widget_glass works around, same one-line way out.
# 95: bar_opacity and dock_opacity above go from 0.05 to `auto`. 94's own
#   comment explained why the number was needed and is now half wrong: the
#   BAR's fallback already worked (theme.json's barAlpha, synui 469+), and only
#   the DOCK's genuinely had none — BarConfig.dockOpacity's declared default
#   was a flat 0.72 whatever theme was on screen. synui 483 gives it the same
#   theme.json fallback the bar already had (a new dockAlpha field), so `auto`
#   resolves correctly on the very first login and neither line needs to spell
#   out a number that only happened to match the theme's own answer.
# 98: a tty-less sudo could lock the account until the next reboot.
#   pam_faillock counts a failed PAM CONVERSATION as a wrong password, and a
#   sudo with no controlling tty and no askpass cannot prompt at all — pam_unix
#   logs "auth could not identify password" and a failure is recorded against a
#   user who never typed anything. Three of those and the account is locked.
#   ⚠ The lock is on the ACCOUNT, not on sudo. greetd and synui-lock reach this
#   same stack — synui's own /etc/pam.d/synui-lock includes login deliberately,
#   on the grounds that a lock weaker than the login behind it is not a lock —
#   so what it looks like from the chair is a LOGIN SCREEN REJECTING A CORRECT
#   PASSWORD, with nothing on it to say why. Twice on this machine
#   (2026-08-21 12:51, 2026-08-26 18:34), both ending in a reboot.
#   Both ended in a reboot because nothing else cleared it. pambase ships
#   `auth required pam_faillock.so preauth`, and `required` does not
#   short-circuit: a retry against an ALREADY-locked account still falls
#   through to pam_unix and then to `[default=die] pam_faillock authfail`,
#   which appends a NEW failure record carrying a fresh timestamp. Every
#   attempt pushed unlock_time forward, so the 600s never elapsed and only a
#   reboot ended it — the tallies live on tmpfs under /run/faillock. Retrying
#   was the thing keeping it locked, which is what a locked-out person does.
#   `requisite` aborts the stack the moment preauth reports the lock, so
#   nothing further is written and the window expires on its own. pambase's own
#   comment on the line below it names requisite as the supported alternative;
#   the visible trade is that a locked account is TOLD so rather than asked for
#   a password that cannot work.
#   deny/fail_interval/unlock_time go into faillock.conf explicitly (5/900/600)
#   instead of staying implicit, because those numbers govern the machine and
#   should be readable on it. deny was effectively 3 — three typos at the lock
#   screen took the seat.
#   ⚠ pam_faillock_configure() sits ABOVE the test seam and reports whether the
#   stack READS requisite afterwards, not whether sed ran. Both files belong to
#   other packages — system-auth to pambase, faillock.conf to pam — and
#   upstream rewords them; an anchor that has moved makes sed match nothing,
#   change nothing and exit 0, which is a default that silently did not apply
#   on every install until somebody looks. Same shape as
#   pacman_conf_enable_candy() and candy_test.sh.
#   ⚠ tests/faillock_test.sh asserts authfail and authsucc are NOT caught by
#   that regex. A greedy match there would stop failures being counted at all
#   and stop a success clearing the tally — a lockout that never happens and
#   one that never ends, from one careless character.
#   ⚠ Existing machines are NOT touched. Both files are pacman BACKUP files, so
#   an installed system carries its own copies forward and syn-update has no
#   business rewriting either. This is the fresh-install default only; an
#   existing box needs the one-line sed by hand.
# 99: the drop-in that lets game mode quiet synapse_kmod is verified like the
#   rest of them. 79 wrote /etc/sudoers.d/zz-synui-kmod-events and did not add
#   it to either verify loop below the chroot block, so for twenty releases the
#   one check standing between a fresh install and a game mode that cannot
#   quiet the module did not look at it. The loops exist BECAUSE that block is
#   masked and has failed silently twice; a name missing from them is that same
#   hole one layer down, and the failure is quiet in exactly the same way —
#   synui's spawn is fire-and-forget, so the whole trace is one "a password is
#   required" line per toggle in the journal.
#   ⚠ tests/sudoers_test.sh SCRAPES the script and asserts the written list and
#   the two verified lists are one list, rather than restating the names a
#   third time — a third copy is the same bug again. It also asserts every
#   NOPASSWD name sorts after 'wheel' (sudo takes the LAST match, and
#   /etc/sudoers.d/wheel is the blanket rule with no NOPASSWD) and that every
#   drop-in is chmod 440, which visudo -c fails the whole ruleset over.
#   Verified to FAIL when the name is taken back out of the loops.
#   ⚠ Existing machines are NOT fixed by this. The drop-in is written by the
#   INSTALLER, so a box installed before 79 never had it and syn-update does
#   not add installer-written files; it needs writing by hand, with visudo -c
#   before and after.
# 100: A FRESH INSTALL CARRIED THREE TERMINALS AND OPENED ONE OF THEM. kitty
#   was pacstrapped in the base set from the days it was the default; syntty
#   replaced it as the default in synui 359 and as a hard dependency shortly
#   after, and this list went on carrying 65 MiB that nothing opens, nothing
#   names, and no fresh synuirc mentions.
#   ⛔ AND ON THE ISO IT WAS WORSE THAN DEAD WEIGHT: kitty needs a working
#   OpenGL context, which is the one thing a live image cannot count on — an
#   unfamiliar GPU, a driver that did not load, a VM with no acceleration. The
#   comment beside it there still read "kitty stays the default terminal",
#   which had not been true for a hundred and fifty releases.
#   Two ship now. syntty is the default and links no GL at all; foot stays as
#   the rescue, and ⚠ THE REASON IT STAYS CHANGED — it used to be "kitty needs
#   OpenGL and foot does not", which stopped being the point the day syntty
#   became the default, because syntty does not either. What foot is now is a
#   SECOND IMPLEMENTATION: 793 KiB of somebody else's code, so a bug that stops
#   syntty opening still leaves a way to get a prompt and fix it.
#   ⚠ kitty's CONFIG IS STILL WRITTEN, deliberately. It is one `synpkg install
#   kitty` away, and a terminal somebody chooses to add should land on the same
#   palette as the two that shipped rather than on kitty's own defaults.
#   ⚠ AND EVERY FALLBACK CHAIN STILL NAMES IT. A box installed before this has
#   kitty and no syntty; a chain that dropped the name would leave it with no
#   terminal at all.
#   Seven checks in layout_test.sh, against BOTH lists — the installer's and
#   the ISO's are independent collectors and have drifted before.
pkgrel=103
pkgdesc="SynapseOS disk installer"
arch=('any')
license=('GPL-2.0-or-later')
# cryptsetup: the optional LUKS2 install path, and syn-crypt afterwards.
depends=('bash' 'parted' 'e2fsprogs' 'dosfstools' 'arch-install-scripts' 'grub'
         'cryptsetup' 'util-linux')
# The graphical installer is quickshell rendering one QML file. It is an
# optdepend rather than a depend because the TEXT installer is the one that
# must work everywhere — on a headless box, over SSH, and on a machine whose
# GPU cannot start a compositor, which is exactly when someone is installing.
optdepends=('quickshell: the graphical installer (syn-install-gui)')

# FLAT, next to the PKGBUILD. makepkg resolves a local source entry to its
# BASENAME and looks for it beside the PKGBUILD, so `data/syn-install-gui.qml`
# fails with "not found in the build directory and is not a URL" — which is
# exactly how syn-update's first build of this package died. The name::url
# rename syntax is URL-only, so there is no way to keep the subdirectory.
# syn/ carries the same scar (nix-flake.nix, nix-home.nix).
#
# The tarball-based components (synui, synpkg, syn-disks) are unaffected and do
# use data/ — their paths live inside the tarball. This one builds in place
# from local files.
source=("syn-install.sh"
        "syn-crypt.sh"
        "syn-install-gui.sh"
        "syn-install-gui.qml")
sha256sums=('SKIP'
            'SKIP'
            'SKIP'
            'SKIP')

package() {
    install -Dm755 "$srcdir/syn-install.sh" "$pkgdir/usr/bin/syn-install"
    install -Dm755 "$srcdir/syn-crypt.sh"   "$pkgdir/usr/bin/syn-crypt"

    # The window and its launcher. The QML is data, not a program: it is
    # rendered by quickshell and has no execute bit for the same reason
    # syn-disks' and synpkg's do not.
    install -Dm755 "$srcdir/syn-install-gui.sh" "$pkgdir/usr/bin/syn-install-gui"
    install -Dm644 "$srcdir/syn-install-gui.qml" \
        "$pkgdir/usr/share/syn-install/syn-install-gui.qml"
}
